{{- /* Every cell comes from RepoView.cell, which decides the words for each
state in one place rather than once per column. A failed run and a repo nobody
has ever collected both say "not collected"; a signal nobody configured says
"not measured"; a measured signal with no baseline says "no baseline yet". None
of those branches lives here any more, which is why adding a signal is a
registry row rather than another arm of a template conditional.
The rule those words enforce is unchanged: a failed run came back with no
metrics, so every count on it is a Go zero rather than a measurement, and
printing them would claim the repo sits at 0.0% with every package tested. */}}
{{- range .Repos }}
| {{ .Name }} |{{ range .TableSignals }} {{ .Value }} | {{ .Delta }} |{{ end }} {{ if .CollectedAt }}{{ .CollectedAt }}{{ else }}never{{ end }}{{ if .EnvWarned }}, toolchain changed{{ end }}{{ if .EnvUnknownWarned }}, toolchain unidentified{{ end }}{{ if .DirtyWarned }}, uncommitted changes{{ end }} |
{{- end }}

Coverage is covered over total across the whole repo, so a big package or file counts for more than a small one. Statement coverage counts statements from a Go profile and line coverage counts lines from an LCOV tracefile; several statements on one source line collapse to one line, so the two are different units and neither column is derivable from the other.
{{ if anyEnvWarned .Repos }}
A row marked toolchain changed had its two snapshots taken under different toolchains, so some of that move is not code. It is flagged whether or not the repo moved enough to lead the report.
{{ end -}}
{{ if anyEnvUnknownWarned .Repos }}
A row marked toolchain unidentified is one where nothing established which runtime the numbers were measured under, so an upgrade between the two snapshots would not be flagged. Give the repo a fingerprint command in the config to fix it.
{{ end -}}
{{ if anyDirtyWarned .Repos }}
A row marked uncommitted changes was measured over a working tree with uncommitted work in it, so its numbers belong to no commit and nobody can reproduce them from one. It says nothing about the comparison, only about the newer snapshot, so a repo on its first ever run carries it too.
{{ end -}}
